<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haakon the Good</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 920–961</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Haakon I of Norway, Haakon Adalsteinsfostre</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Haakon the Good (c. 920–961) was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from about 934, the youngest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a>. Raised as a Christian at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, he returned to displace his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-bloodaxe">Eric Bloodaxe</a>. Tradition credits him with organising the law assemblies and the naval levy, the leidang, but his attempt to introduce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> failed. He died after the Battle of Fitjar against Eric's sons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haakon-the-good.jpg" alt="Haakon the Good" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Nicolai Arbo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_Nicolai_Arbo-Haakon_den_gode.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
